  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "None noted. The main paper is present; it refers to supplementary material that is not included.",
    "relation": "discusses",
    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "The study analyzes monosemanticity and temporal coherence of SAE features in video models, not whether individual neurons have unrelated meanings. Its measurements do not test the claim versions about neuron polysemanticity.",
    "gate_note": "Table 1 measures MonoSemanticity scores of SAE features under different similarity backbones. These are results on a related but different object from individual neurons, so they are recorded under fits_no_version rather than linked to a claim version. Other reported results concern reconstruction, temporal coherence, action probes, ablations, and retrieval, not neuron polysemanticity.",
    "test_reason": "The study trains TopK SAEs on frozen DINOv2 and VideoMAE video features and scores SAE features, not individual neurons. It never measures whether backbone neurons respond to unrelated inputs, what causes such responses, or how their share varies. It mentions monosemanticity only as a property of SAE dictionaries. The MS-metric artifact concerns SAE-feature scoring across backbones, a related but different construct from neuron polysemanticity (V7), so it is recorded under fits_no_version.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Correct. The authors conclude that standard SAEs give monosemantic video features but destroy temporal coherence (lag-1 down 36%). Contrastive weighting gives a controllable reconstruction–coherence trade-off. The own-space MS metric carries a backbone-alignment artifact that disappears under CLIP similarity.",
    "recipe": "none",
    "recipe_differences": "The study trains TopK SAEs on video-backbone representations and evaluates SAE features; it does not reproduce a method for identifying or testing polysemantic individual neurons.",
    "method_sensitivity": "The SAE-feature MonoSemanticity scores vary with the similarity backbone: the own-space scores differ substantially across DINOv2 and VideoMAE, while CLIP-space scores are similar across the two backbones.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "Own-space MS for SAE features is 0.317–0.366 for DINOv2 and 0.969–0.974 for VideoMAE. Under CLIP ViT-B/32 similarity, every entry falls to 0.668–0.683, closing the cross-backbone gap.",
        "proposed_claim": "MonoSemanticity scores of SAE features depend on the similarity space used to score them, so they are comparable within a backbone but not across backbones.",
